backend-editor-title = Backend configuration
backend-editor-add = Add backend
backend-editor-edit = Edit backend
backend-editor-intro = Configure one service endpoint and its authentication identity.
backend-editor-back = Back to domain
backend-editor-identity = Service and selection
backend-editor-id = Backend ID
backend-editor-id-help = Stable identifier, unique within this domain. Use letters, digits, hyphens or underscores. Existing IDs cannot be renamed.
backend-editor-protocol = Protocol and service
backend-editor-protocol-help = IMAP, POP3 and JMAP provide mail; EWS provides mail and submission; SMTP provides submission; ManageSieve provides server rules. JMAP proxy and mail engine modes cannot yet be mixed in one server.
backend-editor-priority = Priority
backend-editor-priority-help = Lower numbers win. A preferred backend wins first; ties use the backend ID. Selection never retries credentials on another backend.
backend-editor-enabled = Enabled
backend-editor-enabled-help = Disabled backends are excluded from selection. If no mail backend remains enabled, sign-in is refused; there is no implicit fallback.
backend-editor-preferred = Preferred for this service
backend-editor-preferred-help = Prefer this backend over other priorities. Saving clears the previous preferred backend for this service.
backend-editor-connection = Connection
backend-editor-host = Hostname or IP address
backend-editor-host-help = A hostname is resolved and checked on every new connection, so DNS updates take effect. A literal IP address fixes the destination. Enter no scheme, path or port here.
backend-editor-port = Port
backend-editor-port-help = The TCP port for this endpoint, from 1 to 65535. Match the server's TLS or STARTTLS listener.
backend-editor-security = Connection security
backend-editor-security-help = TLS encrypts immediately. STARTTLS requires a successful upgrade before authentication. Certificate validation is strict; failures do not downgrade the connection.
backend-editor-plaintext = Plaintext (explicit insecure choice)
backend-editor-plaintext-warning = Plaintext sends credentials and mail without transport encryption. Choose it only for an explicitly trusted local endpoint.
backend-editor-private = Permit this endpoint on a private network
backend-editor-private-help = Allows private LAN and loopback addresses only for this configured endpoint. Metadata, link-local and embedded blocked addresses remain refused.
backend-editor-advanced-ready = Advanced transport security is available for supported protocols.
backend-editor-advanced-unavailable = Automatic security negotiation, custom certificate authorities and TLS name overrides are not available until the server transport supports them. SSH transport is not yet connected.
backend-editor-session-url = JMAP session URL
backend-editor-session-help = The operator-approved HTTP(S) session discovery URL. Do not include credentials, fragments or a mailbox URL.
backend-editor-auth = Authentication
backend-editor-auth-help = These mail transports currently use the user's password. OAuth and client-certificate authentication require separate runtime support and are not selectable here.
backend-editor-username-format = Authentication username format
backend-editor-username-help = Only the username sent to this backend changes. Account IDs, email identities, aliases and policy subjects remain unchanged. JMAP currently uses the full email identity.
backend-editor-format-fullEmail = Full email address
backend-editor-format-localUser = Local username
backend-editor-format-domainUser = DOMAIN\username
backend-editor-format-userRealm = username@realm
backend-editor-format-custom = Custom template
backend-editor-auth-domain = Authentication domain
backend-editor-auth-domain-help = The directory or Windows domain placed before the backslash, for example CORP. This does not change the email domain.
backend-editor-realm = Authentication realm
backend-editor-realm-help = The server's explicit authentication realm. Required for username@realm or a custom realm token.
backend-editor-template = Username template
backend-editor-template-help = Use the user, domain, email and optional realm tokens in braces. Include user or email. Templates are text substitution only, never executable code.
backend-editor-example = Example email identity
backend-editor-example-help = Preview the exact authentication username without sending credentials or contacting any upstream server.
backend-editor-preview-username = Preview authentication username
backend-editor-preview-result = Authentication username
backend-editor-save-help = Saving changes the domain's selected configuration. Existing accounts must sign in again if their selected authority or authentication policy changes.
backend-editor-save = Save backend
backend-list-title = Service backends
backend-list-help = Each service selects its preferred enabled backend, then the lowest priority and ID. Credentials are never retried against another backend.
backend-list-failover = Fail over to the next mail backend when the selected one cannot be reached
backend-list-failover-help = Applies to IMAP and POP3. Before signing in, Mailwoman checks the selected backend without credentials; on a DNS, connection, timeout, TLS or certificate failure it checks the next enabled backend of the same protocol, up to three in total. A rejected password is never tried on another backend. A failed backend is skipped for 30 seconds, doubling per failure up to 15 minutes, then tried first again. Every failover is written to the application log. All backends in the chain must use the same connection security, authentication and username format, and turning this on asks existing accounts to sign in again.
backend-list-selected = Selected
backend-list-none = No enabled backend selected
backend-list-upgrade = Adding or editing a backend upgrades the domain to explicit endpoint selection. Mail no longer falls back to deployment defaults.
backend-list-edit = Edit
backend-list-add = Add backend
backend-list-role-mail = Mail access
backend-list-role-submission = Message submission
backend-list-role-rules = Server rules
backend-editor-forbidden = Your admin role cannot change backend connections.
backend-editor-missing = This backend no longer exists. Return to the domain and reload its configuration.
backend-security-title = Advanced transport security
backend-security-customize = Customize transport security
backend-security-customize-help = Override the simple TLS setting with explicit negotiation and certificate trust rules for this endpoint only.
backend-security-mode = Security selection
backend-security-fixed = Fixed method
backend-security-auto = Auto, with explicit priority
backend-security-mode-help = Fixed uses one method. Auto probes allowed methods before authentication and only advances after an explicit unsupported-method response. Certificate errors, timeouts and authentication failures never trigger fallback.
backend-security-order = Method priority { $index }
backend-security-order-help = Earlier methods win. Do not repeat methods. Empty later slots are unused. Plaintext needs explicit permission below.
backend-security-unused = Unused
backend-security-plaintext = Explicitly permit plaintext
backend-security-plaintext-help = This does not add plaintext automatically. It only permits an explicitly listed plaintext method. Never use it across an untrusted network.
backend-security-server-name = TLS server name override
backend-security-server-name-help = Optional certificate hostname and SNI, for example mail.example.com when connecting to a fixed IP address. Verification remains mandatory and must match this name.
backend-security-ca = Additional trusted CA certificates (PEM)
backend-security-ca-help = Optional public CA certificates for an internal certificate authority. System public roots remain trusted. No private keys; maximum 64 KiB.
backend-security-verify = Certificate signatures, validity and server names are always verified. There is no disable-verification switch.
backend-auth-mechanisms-title = Password authentication methods
backend-auth-customize = Customize authentication methods
backend-auth-customize-help = Limit the password mechanisms this endpoint may use. Without this override, the protocol's existing authentication behavior is preserved.
backend-auth-unavailable = Explicit authentication selection is unavailable on this server or protocol. OAuth tokens and client certificates are not password mechanisms.
backend-auth-mode = Authentication selection
backend-auth-mode-help = Fixed requires one method. Auto chooses the first allowed method supported on the secured connection. SASL methods must be advertised; standard IMAP LOGIN and legacy POP3 USER/PASS follow their protocol's command rules. A rejected attempt never retries another method or backend.
backend-auth-priority = Authentication priority { $index }
backend-auth-priority-help = Earlier methods win. Do not repeat methods. Empty later slots are unused. Unsupported methods fail before credentials are sent.
backend-auth-plus-help = SCRAM-PLUS requires a verified TLS connection with channel binding and server support. ManageSieve does not currently support PLUS. Plain and LOGIN credentials rely on the configured transport for confidentiality.
backend-auth-mechanism-plain = SASL PLAIN
backend-auth-mechanism-login = SASL LOGIN
backend-auth-mechanism-scram-sha-256 = SCRAM-SHA-256
backend-auth-mechanism-scram-sha-256-plus = SCRAM-SHA-256-PLUS
backend-auth-mechanism-imap-login = IMAP LOGIN command
backend-auth-mechanism-pop3-user-pass = POP3 USER/PASS commands

backend-ews-endpoint = Exchange Web Services endpoint
backend-ews-endpoint-help = Exact HTTPS endpoint, for example https://exchange.example.org/EWS/Exchange.asmx. Credentials, query strings and fragments are not allowed in the URL.
backend-ews-auth = EWS authentication
backend-ews-basic = HTTPS Basic
backend-ews-auth-help = Uses the signing-in user's password over verified TLS. OAuth, NTLM and MAPI are not supported by this domain integration.
backend-ews-ca = Additional origin CA certificates (optional)
backend-ews-ca-help = PEM certificates only, at most 8 certificates and 64 KiB. No private keys. Hostname and certificate-chain verification remain enabled; proxy trust is unchanged. Leave empty for system trust.
backend-ews-routing-help = EWS provides mail submission itself. Disable separate SMTP and Sieve backends when EWS is selected. Private servers require an explicit Plugins destination permission in Outbound routing; this form grants no network exception.
backend-ews-diagnostics-help = Saving validates configuration, not credentials or service availability. Unauthenticated diagnostics cannot test EWS; a successful authenticated provider connection is required.
backend-ews-invalid = Enter a valid HTTPS EWS endpoint without credentials, query or fragment, using Basic authentication and no socket or JMAP configuration.
backend-ews-invalid-ca = Enter 1–8 PEM CA certificates, no private keys, within 64 KiB. The server also validates the certificates.
backend-ews-service-conflict = The selected EWS backend supplies submission. Disable the active SMTP and Sieve backends, or select another mail backend, before saving.
